Controlled diversity: Templates ensure balanced coverage of types (e.g. by up-weighting rare classes). Synthetic expansions can target low-resource categories for fairness.	Factuality issues: Rule-based or model-based generation can introduce hallucinations or nonsensical outputs if templates aren’t precise.
Scalability and reproducibility: Automated templates generate data at scale, and fixed seeds + versioning ensure full reproducibility.	Bias amplification: Templates drawn from a canonical library may inherit bias (e.g. cultural bias in proverbs). Systematic review needed to catch stereotypes.
Privacy and compliance: Using abstract or anonymized content avoids real personal data, reducing privacy risks.	Overfitting to templates: A limited template pool may produce repetitive or formulaic examples, limiting generalization.
Traceability: Embedding metadata (template ID, seed, source) makes lineage explicit.	Duplication: If uniqueness checks fail, duplicate or near-duplicate examples can slip through, harming dataset quality.
Quality control: Schema enforcement and integrity checks (e.g. JSON validation) catch many errors upfront.	Complex constraint enforcement: Managing many max_uses and interdependent rules can cause generation failures if not carefully tuned.

Recommendations
	•	Modularize the pipeline code into well-defined stages (data load, render, validate, output) with clear interfaces. Use version control (Git) and CI to test changes.
	•	Schema-driven validation: Employ JSON Schema or Pydantic models to catch malformed records automatically.
	•	Logging: Implement detailed logging for both successful generations and rejections. Include example IDs and template references in logs for audit.
	•	Dataset versioning: Use content hashes and manifest files to version outputs. Consider DVC or similar tools to snapshot JSONL datasets.
	•	Seed management: Fix random seeds and log them in metadata. Use deterministic shuffling so that any rerun yields the same synthetic set.
	•	Metrics and drift: Periodically compute simple stats (vocabulary, output lengths) to detect distribution shifts over regeneration runs.

By enforcing a strict schema and logging pipeline metadata, KALAXI can ensure each example is traceable and valid. Creative augmentation (template fusion, context chaining, tiered difficulty) can further enrich the dataset. All new mechanics should be implemented with accompanying checks (unit tests, schema validation) to maintain integrity.

Sources: Modern synthetic data pipelines emphasize modular, block-based design, strict JSON schema enforcement, and robust versioning/lineage practices. Synthetic data provides scale and balance but must guard against hallucination and bias. Techniques like difficulty-graded generation offer one strategy to expand and validate synthetic examples.

